// check: refuse E_EffectMismatch
// A cut whose end a call hands past the list stays open. The guard proves
// `0 <= b`, so the start and the order of the bounds hold, but the one call
// hands `b` the value 5 for a list of three. A call that fails a bound
// refutes nothing, since the guard may keep it from reaching the cut, so the
// cut keeps the Trap its check can raise and `!Trap` is refused.
fn upto(xs, b) with !Trap = if 0 <= b { slice(xs, 0, b) } else { [] }

fn main() = len(upto([1, 2, 3], 5))
